```c
static size_t
is_codefence(uint8_t *data, size_t size, size_t *width, uint8_t *chr)
{
    size_t i = 0, n = 1;
    uint8_t c;

    /* skipping initial spaces */
    if (size < 3)
        return 0;

    if (data[0] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[1] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[2] == ' ') { i++; } } }

    /* looking at the hrule uint8_t */
    c = data[i];
    if (i + 2 >= size || !(c=='~' || c=='`'))
        return 0;

    /* the fence must be that same character */
    while (++i < size && data[i] == c)
        ++n;

    if (n < 3)
        return 0;

    if (width) *width = n;
    if (chr) *chr = c;
    return i;
}
```

Wait, I just noticed something. The check `if (i + 2 >= size || !(c=='~' || c=='`'))` uses `i + 2 >= size`. But `i` could be 0, 1, 2, or 3. If `i = 3` and `size = 4`, then `i + 2 = 5 >= 4` is true, so it returns 0. But `c = data[3]` was already read (in bounds since `size = 4`).

What if `i = 3` and `size = 3`? Then `i + 2 = 5 >= 3` is true, so it returns 0. But `c = data[3]` was already read (OOB).

So the OOB read is always at `data[3]` when `size = 3` and all 3 bytes are spaces. This is a 1-byte OOB read.

Now, the question is: can I make this 1-byte OOB read happen at a buffer boundary? Let me think about how the `text` buffer is allocated...

The `text` buffer is created with `hoedown_buffer_new(64)` and grown to `ib->size` bytes. The growth is in multiples of 64. So `text->asize` is always a multiple of 64.

If `ib->size = 64`, then `text->asize = 64`. The `text` buffer has exactly 64 bytes of allocated space. If the data in `text` is exactly 64 bytes, and the last line is `   ` (3 spaces) at position 61-63, then `is_codefence` would read `text->data[64]` which is past the allocated buffer!

Wait, but `text->size` is the size of the copied data, not `text->asize`. If `ib->size = 64`, then `text->asize = 64`. The data copied into `text` is at most 64 bytes. If the data is exactly 64 bytes, then `text->size = 64`. The last byte is at `text->data[63]`.

If the last line is `   ` (3 spaces) at position 61-63, then `is_codefence` is called with `data = text->data + 61` and `size = 3`. `data[3] = text->data[64]` which is past the allocated buffer!

